          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"alcott-little-women-9bf6f2655be1fc50\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.98,\"analysis\":\"Jo directly avows her sisterly love for Beth, specifically praising Beth’s effort to overcome her shyness. The utterance presents a loving emotional attitude as true. It encourages and reassures Beth, but does not undertake a commitment, and there is no positive evidence that the words are a reflex-like affective discharge rather than an affectionate avowal.\",\"ambiguity\":null},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Jo directly tells Beth, “I love you for it,” during their conversation about Beth overcoming her shyness.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The avowal is embedded in Jo’s praise of Beth’s decision to attend the outing despite her shyness. “For it” identifies Beth’s effort as the immediate ground of Jo’s affectionate commendation. The speech also functions as encouragement—explicitly compared with the lift supplied by a cheery word—but that interpersonal effect does not by itself create performative or expressive/reflexive force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“You do try to fight off your shyness, and I love you for it.”\",\"supports\":\"The explicit first-person avowal, with Beth’s effort supplied as its reason, strongly supports truth-conditional force.\",\"confidence\":1.0},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“That’s my good girl” and “a cheery word kind of gives a lift.”\",\"supports\":\"The surrounding praise frames the love utterance as affectionate encouragement rather than a relational undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Beth has just agreed to attend after asking Jo to take care of her.\",\"supports\":\"The immediate exchange identifies Beth as the addressee and shows that Jo is commending her attempt to resist shyness.\",\"confidence\":0.98}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core avowal. Its encouraging delivery and sisterly context require no additional P, E, or O classification.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
